  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+4z & =12 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+z) & =12 \\\\ {z}^{2}+z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=\\frac{1}{2},q=\\frac{13}{4},$ and $p+q=\\frac{15}{4}.$ \n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\ |z+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
